Subject: Report a class's member function templates through `ParseCallbacks`

bindgen does not parse a member function template as a member of its class at
all. libclang reports one as a `CXCursor_FunctionTemplate` rather than as a
`CXCursor_CXXMethod`, and the visitor in `CompInfo::from_ty` lists that cursor
among the ones it passes over, so such a member reaches no IR, no callback and
no output. Nothing bindgen generates distinguishes a class which declares one
from a class which declares none.

Generating nothing for one is right - a template has no monomorphization to
emit - and this is a step in front of where that decision is usually made.
`--report-template-member-functions` does not reach these either: it keeps the
*ordinary* members of a class template, which are discarded later and for a
different reason, and a member function template of an ordinary class was never
in front of that check to begin with.

`--report-member-function-templates` keeps what C++ said about each of them and
reports it through `denote_member_function_template`: the name, which kind of
member function the template declares, the constness, the access, and how many
template parameters the member declares of its own. A consumer which writes its
own C++ can call one, choosing the arguments itself - that is what its own C++
is for - and a consumer which cannot still has to be able to say the member is
there, rather than pass over it as silently as bindgen did.

`clang_getTemplateCursorKind` is what makes the cursor readable. It answers with
the kind the declaration would have had had it not been a template, and among a
class's children it answers one of three: `CXCursor_CXXMethod` for a member
function template, `CXCursor_Constructor` for a member template constructor, and
`CXCursor_ConversionFunction` for a conversion function template. The last is
passed over, where a conversion function of any other kind already is, and could
not be reported as it stands in any case: libclang spells one `operator
type-parameter-0-0` rather than `operator T`.

Those three are all C++ allows here - [temp.mem]/2 rules out a destructor
template and [temp.mem]/3 a virtual member function template - so the two taken
are every kind reported, and the one left out is left out deliberately rather
than overlooked. Neither can another kind of template be mistaken for one,
libclang giving a member class template `CXCursor_ClassTemplate`, a member alias
template `CXCursor_TypeAliasTemplateDecl` and a member variable template
`CXCursor_UnexposedDecl`; and a friend function template is not a child of the
class at all, hanging under a `CXCursor_FriendDecl` with the translation unit
for its semantic parent. `clang_CXXMethod_isStatic` and
`clang_CXXMethod_isConst` answer for these cursors exactly as they do for a
`CXCursor_CXXMethod`.

No signature is reported, and the sibling option's reason is not this one. That
one withholds a signature where it would name a parameter of the *class*, which
an instantiation supplies; here the parameters are the member's own, and no
instantiation of the class supplies those. Not every such signature names one -
`template<class T = int> int f(int)` names none - so reporting the ones which do
not would be possible and is simply a feature of its own; what this reports is
that the member is there and what C++ declared it as.

The parameters are counted instead. The count is of what C++ declared rather than
of what a caller must write - deduction and default template arguments may supply
them - and a parameter pack counts as the one parameter C++ declared it as,
libclang exposing no test for a pack.

The report is made from `CompInfo::codegen`, beside the reports every class
makes, rather than from the point codegen gives up on a class template's
members. A member function template belongs to an ordinary class as readily as
to a class template and is generated for neither, so the place which speaks only
for the class codegen gave up on would report half of them. A member whose kind
`--generate` leaves out is not reported, as for any other function; there is no
per-member blocklist to consult, a member function template never becoming an
item for a blocklist to match, and a blocklisted class does not reach codegen at
all.

Opt-in because keeping these is not free - a header which uses the standard
library declares a great many - and because a report made unconditionally would
be a new one for every consumer which already implements `ParseCallbacks`. With
the option off nothing changes at all.

